{0 Mehari}

Mehari is a cross-platform library for building Gemini servers. It fully
implements the
{{:https://geminiprotocol.net/docs/protocol-specification.gmi} Gemini protocol specification}.
It offers a simple and clean interface to create complete Gemini web apps.
It takes inspiration from {{:https://github.com/aantron/dream} Dream}, a
tidy, feature-complete Web framework.

Consult the {!section-tutorial} and
{{:https://github.com/Psi-Prod/Mehari/tree/master/examples} examples}.

- {{:https://github.com/Psi-Prod/Mehari} Homepage}
- {{:https://github.com/Psi-Prod/Mehari/issues} Bugtracker}

{1 Interface}

Mehari provides several packages:

  - {!Mehari} provides the core abstraction, it does not depend on any platform
    code, and does not interact with the environment.

  - {{:../mehari-mirage/index.html} Mehari_mirage} is a server implementation
    compatible with Mirage.

  - {{:../mehari-miou/index.html} Mehari_miou} uses the
    {{:https://robur-coop.github.io/miou/} Miou} library, and
    specifically the UNIX bindings.

{1:tutorial Tutorial}

{2 Respond to request}

The first form of abstraction is the {!type:Mehari.handler} which is essentially
an asynchronous function
from {!type:Mehari.request} to {!type:Mehari.response}.
This is the simplest possible handler that responds
to all requests in the same way:

{@ocaml[(fun req -> Mehari.Response.text "Hello from Mehari")]}

As handlers take a {!Mehari.type-request} in parameter we can operate on them.
Here we retrieve the URL of the client's request and return it:

{@ocaml[(fun req -> Mehari.Request.uri req |> Uri.to_string |> Mehari.Response.text)]}

See {!Mehari.section-request} section to consult all client request related
functions.

{3 Status}

Mehari provides its own representation of status codes as described in the
{{:Gemini protocol specification} gemini specification}. Take a look at the
signature of {!val:Mehari.Response.respond}:

{@ocaml[val respond : 'a status -> 'a -> response]}

Furthermore, the status [input] and [success] are defined as follows:

{@ocaml[val input : string status]}
{@ocaml[val success : Body.t -> Mime.t status]}

The inhabitant type of {!Mehari.Status.t} carries the information of what
is necessary for the creation of an associated response. In the case of an
input response, a [string] is needed:

{@ocaml[
let input_resp =
  Mehari.(Response.respond Status.input) "Enter a message"
]}

In the same way, a {!type:Mehari.Body.t} and a {!type:Mehari.Mime.t} are required
to make a successful response:

{@ocaml[
open Mehari

let successful_resp =
  let body = Body.string "A successful response" in
  Response.respond (Status.success body) (Mime.gemini ())
]}

See {!Mehari.section-status} for the complete list of status. Other functions
described in {!Mehari.section-response} section are mostly convenient functions
built on top of {!val:Mehari.Response.respond}.

{3 Body}

Successful responses are accompanied by a {!type:Mehari.Body.t}.
They are many methods to create a body, for example from a string:

{@ocaml[
let body = Mehari.Body.string "A response body"
]}

Or from a Gemtext object:
{@ocaml[
let body =
  let open Mehari in
  Response.gemtext
    Gemtext.[
      heading `H1 "Thought on Gemtext markup";
      list_item "Date: February 2021";
      list_item "Tags: gemini, reviews";
      newline;
      heading `H2 "Introduction";
      newline;
      quote "The format permits richer typographic possibilities than the plain text of Gopher, but remains extremely easy to parse.";
      text "Here is an example of a Python parser that demonstrates the truth of that statement:";
      preformat "..." ~alt:"python"
    ]
]}

Which is rendered as the following Gemtext document:

{@gemtext[
# Thought on Gemtext markup
* Date: February 2021
* Tags: gemini, reviews

## Introduction

> The format permits richer typographic possibilities than the plain text of Gopher, but remains extremely easy to parse.
Here is an example of a Python parser that demonstrates the truth of that statement:
```python
...
```
]}

{4 Data stream response}

Mehari offers ways to keep client connections open forever and stream data in
real time. Be sure to read this quick warning about this approach:
{!Mehari.Response.Body.section-"note-on-data-stream-response"}.

{3 Mime}

{!type:Mehari.Mime.t} describes how the response body
must be interpreted by the client.
You can build your own mime with {!val:Mehari.Mime.make} and specify the
data encoding with the parameter [charset]:

{@ocaml[
let mp3 = Mehari.Mime.make "audio/mp3"
]}

Some common MIME type are predefined. See {!Mehari.section-mime} section.

The [text/gemini] MIME type allows an additional parameter [lang] to specify
the languages used in the document according to the
{{:https://geminiprotocol.net/docs/protocol-specification.gmi} Gemini specification}:

{@ocaml[
let french_ascii_gemini = Mehari.Mime.gemini ~charset:"ascii" ~lang:["fr"] ()
]}

{4 Inference}

{!val:Mehari.Mime.from_filename} enables MIME type infering from a filename.

Mehari also provides an experimental
{{:https://github.com/mirage/conan} Conan} integration via
{!val:Mehari.Mime.from_content} to infer MIME type from a string.

{2:route Routing}

Obviously, the path of a route corresponds to the "path" component of the url
requested by the client. :

{@ocaml[Mehari.(Router.route Path.(~/"var"/"gemini") (fun _ -> ...))]}

In the following snippet, we construct a path to group all URL of form "/articles/xxx":

{@ocaml[
Mehari.(Router.route Path.(~/"articles" /: string) (fun article req ->
  Response.text (Printf.sprintf "Get article %S" article)))
]}

Each path variable used is passed to the handler, this allows type safe routing.
Predefined path variable are provided for all common standard data type
and it is possible to define path variable for your own type with {!Mehari.Path.variables}.
See the full documentation of {!Mehari.Path} for more information.

It is the purpose of {!val:Mehari.Router.router} to group routes together to
produce a bigger handler:

{@ocaml[
let router =
  let open Mehari in
  Router.router [
    Router.route Path.root index_handler;
    Router.route Path.(~/"gemlog") gemlog_handler;
  ]
]}

{2 Advanced routing}

{3 Middleware}

{!type:Mehari.Middleware.t} allows to run code before and after the execution
of another handler and produces a “bigger” {!type:Mehari.handler}.

This example of an incrementable counter shows how to use them:

{@ocaml[
let counter = ref 0

let incr_count handler req =
  incr counter;
  handler req

let router =
  let open Mehari in
  Router.router [
      Router.route Path.root (fun _ ->
        Response.text (Printf.sprintf "Value %i" !counter));
      Router.route Path.(~/"incr") ~middlewares:[incr_count] (fun _ ->
          Response.respond Status.redirect_temp "/");
    ]
]}

{3 Rate limit}

This route is limited to 3 accesses every 5 minutes:

{@ocaml[
open Mehari

let limit = Rate_limit.make ~period:3 5 `Minute

let limited_route = Router.route Path.(~/"stats") ~rate_limit:limit (fun req -> ...)
]}

They are described in depth in section {!Mehari.section-rate_limit}.

{2 Hosting}

By default, the server runs on port [1965].

Use the [openssl] utility to generate a certificate and its associated private key ([cert.pem]
and [key.pem]) for the domain name [localhost]:

{@bash[
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout key.pem -out cert.pem -sha256 -days 365 -nodes --subj "/CN=localhost"
]}

Here is what you have to do to start the server using {!Mehari_miou}:

{@ocaml[
let load_certs ~cert ~priv_key =
  let certs =
    let pem = In_channel.(with_open_text cert input_all) in
    match X509.Certificate.decode_pem_multiple pem with
    | Ok cs -> cs
    | Error (`Msg m) -> invalid_arg ("failed to parse certificates " ^ m)
  and priv_key =
    let pem = In_channel.(with_open_text priv_key input_all) in
    match X509.Private_key.decode_pem pem with
    | Ok key -> key
    | Error (`Msg m) -> invalid_arg ("failed to parse private key " ^ m)
  in
  [ (certs, priv_key) ]

let () =
  Miou_unix.run @@ fun () ->
  Mirage_crypto_rng_unix.use_default ();
  let certs = load_certs ~cert:"cert.pem" ~priv_key:"key.pem" in
  let ip = Ipaddr.(V4 (V4.Prefix.make 8 V4.localhost)) in
  Mehari_miou.run ~certs ip router
]}

{3 Virtual hosting}

Mehari supports virtual hosting using "server name indication" (TLS SNI extension).
{!val:Mehari.Router.virtual_host} takes a list composed of a couple which
represent a domain and his associated handler and produces a "biggest"
{!type:Mehari.handler}.
